          %h2#LieModels Idea
          %p
            Rational homotopy theory is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+topological+spaces"} rational topological spaces
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simply+connected+topological+space"} simply connected
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups"} homotopy groups
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+vector+spaces"}> rational vector spaces
            ).
          %p
            Much of the theory is concerned with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rationalization"} rationalization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups"} homotopy groups
            this means to retain precisely the non-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsion+subgroups"} torsion subgroups
            of the homotopy groups.
          %p
            Two algebraic models of rational homotopy types exist, via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+graded-commutative+algebras"} differential graded-commutative algebras
            (Sullivan model) and via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-Lie+algebras"} dg-Lie algebras
            (Quillen model).
          %p
            This way rational homotopy theory connects
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+graded+algebra"} differential graded algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Kan+correspondence"} Dold-Kan correspondence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sullivan+construction"} Sullivan construction
            in rational homotopy theory connects the conceptually powerful perspective of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            with the computationally powerful perspective of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+graded+algebra"} differential graded algebra
          %p
            Moreover, via the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+hypothesis"} homotopy hypothesis
            the study of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            is connected to that of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} ∞-groupoids
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sullivan+construction"} Sullivan construction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} ∞-groupoids
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+graded+algebra"} differential graded algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "Lie+integration#Henriques"} Henriques 08
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "Lie+integration#Getzler04"} Getzler 09
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            (see there) in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+theory"} ∞-Lie theory
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-%E2%88%9E+algebras"} L-∞ algebras
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p
            There are two established approaches in rational homotopy theory for encoding rational homotopy types in terms of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+theory"} Lie theoretic data
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                In the
                %strong Sullivan approach
                (
                %a{:href => "#Sullivan77"}> Sullivan 77
                ) a 1-connected rational space, in its incarnation as a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve+and+realization"} realizing
                each abstract
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex"} simplex
                by the standard
                = succeed "-simplex" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebra"} dg-algebra
                of
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} differential form
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomial+differential+forms"} polynomial differential forms
                and gluing these dg-algebras all together.
            %li
              %p
                In the
                %strong Quillen approach
                (
                %a{:href => "#Quillen69"}> Quillen 69
                ) the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+space"} loop space
                of the rational space/simplicial set is formed and its
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/H-space"} H-space
                structure strictified to a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+group"} simplicial group
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-Lie+algebra"} dg-Lie algebra
                (a strict
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-infinity-algebra"} L-infinity-algebra
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
                from the
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/primitive+element"} primitive element
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+ring"} group ring
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+Lie+algebra"} simplicial Lie algebra
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moore+complex"} Moore complex
                functor maps this to the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-Lie+algebra"} dg-Lie algebra
                functor that models the rational homotopy type in the Quillen approach.
          %p
            The relation between these two approaches is discussed in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Majewski00"} Majewski 2000
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p#ClassifyingSpacesOfAcyclicGroups
            Rational homotopy theory is mostly restricted to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simply+connected+topological+spaces"} simply connected topological spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/acyclic+groups"} acyclic groups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \Gamma
            is an “acyclic space” in that its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
            vanishes in positive degrees. This means that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sullivan+algebras"} Sullivan algebras
            do not distinguish classifying spaces of acyclic groups from
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contractible+spaces"} contractible spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hurewicz+theorem"} Hurewicz theorem
            asking that all spaces be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simply+connected+topological+space"} simply connected
            precisely makes all “acyclic spaces” be
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contractible+topological+space"} contractible
          %h2#generalities Generalities
          #RationalHomotopyEquivalenceCoincidesWithRationalHomologyEquivalence.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              by
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simply+connected+topological+spaces"} simply connected topological spaces
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon X \to Y
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
              on
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups"} homotopy groups
              after
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+abelian+groups"} tensor product
              with the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℚ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi ℚ
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi ℚ
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi ℚ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\pi_\bullet(f) \otimes \mathbb{Q}
                    \\;\colon\;
                    \\pi_\bullet(X) \otimes \mathbb{Q}
                    \\overset{\simeq}{\longrightarrow}
                    \\pi_\bullet(Y) \otimes \mathbb{Q}
            %p
              precisely if it induces an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
              on
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+homology"} ordinary homology
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi H
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℚ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi H
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℚ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi H
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℚ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    H_\bullet(f,\mathbb{Q})
                    \\;\colon\;
                    H_\bullet(X,\mathbb{Q})
                    \\overset{\simeq}{\longrightarrow}
                    H_\bullet(Y,\mathbb{Q})
                    \\,.
          %p
            This is due to (
            %a{:href => "#Serre53"}> Serre 53
            ).
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              A map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              satisfying the equivalent conditions of def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#RationalHomotopyEquivalenceCoincidesWithRationalHomologyEquivalence"}
              is called a
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+homotopy+equivalence"} rational homotopy equivalence
          %h2#SullivanApproach Sullivan approach
          %p
            We review here the Sullivan approach to rational homotopy theory, where rational topological spaces are modeled by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+graded-commutative+algebras"} differential graded-commutative algebras
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
            with good (cofibrant) representatives being
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sullivan+algebras"} Sullivan algebras
            which are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+duals"} formal duals
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-infinity+algebras"} L-infinity algebras
          %p
            First we discuss how to define an analog of the construction of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+dg-algebra"} de Rham dg-algebra
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            for
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a{:href => "#FormsOnTopSpaces"} Differential forms on topological spaces
              \.
          %p
            These dg-algebras of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/piecewise+polynomial+differential+forms"}> piecewise polynomial
            ” differential forms on a topological space are typically extremely large and unwieldy. Much more tractable dg-algebras are the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/minimal+Sullivan+algebra"} minimal Sullivan algebra
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a{:href => "#SullivanModels"} Sullivan models
              \.
          %p
            The relation between these algebras is that the Sullivan algebras are the cofibrant resolutions of the larger dg-algebras. In order to make this precise, we next recall some basics of topological and algebraic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            in
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#HomotopyTheory"} Homotopy theory
          %p
            Finally we state and discuss the main theorem, that the construction of dg-algebras of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/piecewise+polynomial+differential+forms"} piecewise polynomial differential forms
            on a topological space exhibits an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence
            between the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simply+connected+topological+space"} simply connected
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+topological+spaces"} rational topological spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/of+finite+type"} of finite type
            and that of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/minimal+Sullivan+algebras"} minimal Sullivan algebras
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#SullivanRationalizationAdjunction"} The rationalization adjunction
          %h3#FormsOnTopSpaces Differential forms on topological spaces
          %p
            A central tool in the study of rational topological spaces is an assignment that sends each
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebra"} dg-algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi poly
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^\bullet_{poly}(X)
            that behaves like the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deRham+complex"} deRham dg-algebra
            of a smooth
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+forms"} differential forms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi poly
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^\bullet_{poly}(X)
            consists of
            %em piecewise linear polynomial differential forms
            , in a way described in detail now.
          %p We first discuss this semi-formally in
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#DifferentialFormsOnSpacesIdea"} Idea
          %p and then in more detail in
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a{:href => "#FormsOnSpacesDetails"} Details
              \.
          %h4#DifferentialFormsOnSpacesIdea Idea
          %p
            The construction of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi poly
                    %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^\bullet_{poly}
            is a special case of the following general construction:
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            be any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi PSh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} PSh(C) = [C^{op}, Set]
            for its category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaves
            and let
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi C
                    %mo •
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi dgcAlg
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Omega^\bullet_C : C^{op} \to dgcAlg
          %p
            be
            %em any
            functor to the category of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebra"} dg-algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+and+quantity"} space and quantity
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            as being
            %em test spaces
            and the functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi C
                    %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^\bullet_C
            as assigning to each test space its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deRham+complex"} deRham dg-algebra
          %p
            An example of this construction that is natural from the point of view of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            appears in the study of
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+space"} diffeological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is some subcategory of the category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diff"} Diff
            of smooth
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi C
                    %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^\bullet_C
            is the restriction of the ordinary assignment of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} differential form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi C
                    %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^\bullet_C
            that is non-standard from the point of view of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
          %p
            After postcomposing with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forgetful+functor"} forgetful functor
            that sends each
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebra"} dg-algebra
            to its underlying
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi C
                    %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^\bullet_C
            becomes itself a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi PSh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \in PSh(C)
            any other presheaf, we extend the notation and write
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi C
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi Hom
                    %mrow
                      %mi PSh
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi C
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Omega^\bullet_C(X) := Hom_{PSh(C)}(X, \Omega^\bullet_C)
          %p
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-set"} hom-set
            of presheaves. One checks that this set naturally inherits the structure of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebra"} dg-algebra
            itself, where all operations are given by applying “pointwise” for each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %mi U
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : U \to X
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \in C
            the operations in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi C
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^\bullet_C(U)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi C
                    %mo •
                  %mo :
                  %mi PSh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi dgcAlg
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Omega^\bullet_C : PSh(C) \to dgcAlg^{op}
          %p
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
            of that of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebra"} dg-algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi C
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^\bullet_C(X)
            as the deRham complex of the presheaf
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            as seen by the functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi C
                      %mo •
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi dgcAlg
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^\bullet_C : C \to dgcAlg^{op}
          %p
            By the general discussion at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve+and+realization"} nerve and realization
            this functor has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mi C
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi dgcAlg
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi PSh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_C : dgcAlg^{op} \to PSh(C)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi U
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mi Hom
                    %mi dgcAlg
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi C
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  K_C(A) : U \mapsto Hom_{dgcAlg}(\Omega^\bullet_C(U), A)
                  \\,.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi C
                    %mo •
                  %mo :
                  %mi PSh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ←
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi dgcAlg
                    %mi op
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Omega^\bullet_C : PSh(C) \stackrel{\leftarrow}{\to} : dgcAlg^{op} : K_C
          %p
            is an example for the adjunction induced from a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dualizing+object"} dualizing object
          %p
            See
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+forms+on+presheaves"} differential forms on presheaves
            for more.
          %p For the purpose of rational homotopy theory, consider the following special case of the above general discussion of differential forms on presheaves.
          %p
            Recall that by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+hypothesis"} homotopy hypothesis
            theorem,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            is equivalent to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sSet"} sSet
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+and+quantity"} space and quantity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            is a “generalized space modeled on the
            = succeed "\u201D:" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex+category"} simplex category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
          %p
            Therefore set in the above
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \coloneqq \Delta
          %p
            Now, a simplicial set has no smooth structure in terms of which one could define differential forms globally, but of course each abstract
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex"} simplex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta[k]
            may be regarded as the standard
            = succeed "-simplex" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi Diff
                    %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^k_{Diff}
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diff"} Diff
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi deRham
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi Diff
                      %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^\bullet_{deRham}(\Delta^k_{Diff})
          %p
            The functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi deRham
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi Diff
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi dgcAlg
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^\bullet_{deRham}( \Delta_{Diff}^{(-)} ) : \Delta^{op} \to dgcAlg
            obtained this way is
            %em almost
            the one that – after fed into the above procedure – is used in rational homotopy theory.
          %p
            The only difference is that for the purposes needed here, it is useful to cut down the smooth differential forms to something smaller. Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi poly
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi Diff
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^\bullet_{poly}(\Delta^k_{Diff})
            be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebra"} dg-algebra
            of
            %strong polynomial differential forms
            on the standard
            = succeed "-simplex." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi Diff
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi Diff
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mi poly
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mi Diff
                        %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi poly
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi Diff
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Omega^\bullet(\Delta^k_{Diff}) =
                  C^\infty(\Delta^k_{Diff}) \otimes_{\Omega^0_{poly}(\Delta^k_{Diff})} \Omega^\bullet_{poly}(\Delta^k_{Diff})
                  \\,.
          %p
            For more details see at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+forms+on+simplices"} differential forms on simplices
          %h4#FormsOnSpacesDetails Details
          %p We discuss the definition of polynomial differential forms on topological spaces in more detail.
          #SmoothnSimplex.num_defn
